"{\"content\":\"# Formal Specification — `expr_eval`\\n\\n**Spec version:** 1.0\\n**Specifies:** module `expr_eval.py`, public function `evaluate(expr: str) -> int`\\n**Status:** Normative. “Must” states a requirement; “may” states an allowance;\\nparagraphs marked **[N]** are non-normative commentary.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 1. Purpose, scope, interface\\n\\n`evaluate(expr: str) -> int` parses an arithmetic expression given as a Python\\nstring and returns its integer value, or raises `ValueError` if the input is\\nnot a well-formed expression.\\n\\nSupported constructs (complete list — anything else is out of scope):\\n\\n- non-negative integer literals (decimal, ASCII digits),\\n- binary operators `+` (addition), `-` (subtraction), `*` (multiplication),\\n- unary operators `+` and `-`,\\n- parentheses for grouping,\\n- whitespace between tokens (ignored).\\n\\nExplicitly **out of scope** (their absence is normative): division, modulo,\\nexponentiation, floating-point or other numeral systems, variables, function\\ncalls, comparisons, logical operators, and any other punctuation.\\n\\n**[N]** `_tokenize` and `_Parser` are internal helpers; only the observable\\nbehaviour of `evaluate` is contractual. `evaluate` is a pure function: it must\\nnot mutate its argument, perform I/O, or depend on global mutable state.\\nBehaviour for non-`str` inputs is outside this specification.\\n\\n## 2. Notation\\n\\n- `Σ` — the set of Unicode code points (Python `str` characters); `String = Σ*`.\\n- `ℤ` — the mathematical integers, with the standard operations `+`, `−`, `×`.\\n- Token lists are finite sequences; `ε` is the empty list, `·` is list\\n  concatenation, `head(t)` the first element.\\n- `{ x }` in EBNF means zero or more repetitions of `x`.\\n- `⇓` is the token-coverage relation defined in §7.\\n- All defining equations below are definitional axioms of the semantics.\\n\\n## 3. Lexical structure\\n\\n### 3.1 Character classes\\n\\n- **Digit** `D = { '0', '1', …, '9' }` — ASCII digits only.\\n  **[N]** Non-ASCII Unicode digits (e.g. `٣`) are *invalid characters*, not digits.\\n- **Symbol** `S = { '+', '-', '*', '(', ')' }`.\\n- **Whitespace** `W = { c ∈ Σ : c.isspace() }`, i.e. exactly the characters for\\n  which Python's `str.isspace()` holds.\\n\\n### 3.2 Tokens\\n\\n| Token    | Shape                  | Payload                    |\\n|----------|------------------------|----------------------------|\\n| `INT`    | one or more digits `D` | integer value `val(…)` (§3.4) |\\n| `PLUS`   | `+`                    | —                          |\\n| `MINUS`  | `-`                    | —                          |\\n| `STAR`   | `*`                    | —                          |\\n| `LPAREN` | `(`                    | —                          |\\n| `RPAREN` | `)`                    | —                          |\\n\\nAn `INT` token is identified by its **value**; its source spelling (e.g.\\nleading zeros) is not preserved. Hence `007` and `5` yield the same token.\\n\\n### 3.3 Lexer\\n\\n`lex : String → Token* ⊎ { LexError(c, i) }` is defined by a single strictly\\nleft-to-right scan with index `i`:\\n\\n1. If `i = |s|`, the scan ends and returns the tokens accumulated so far.\\n2. If `s[i] ∈ W`: skip it (`i ← i+1`) and continue.\\n3. If `s[i] ∈ D`: let `j` be the smallest index `> i` with `s[j] ∉ D`\\n   (or `|s|`). Emit **one** `INT` token for the digit run `s[i..j)` (**maximal\\n   munch**), set `i ← j`, and continue.\\n4. If `s[i] ∈ S`: emit the corresponding single-character token, `i ← i+1`.\\n5. Otherwise: fail with `LexError(s[i], i)` where `i` is the index of the\\n   first character that is in none of `W`, `D`, `S`.\\n\\nConsequences (normative): whitespace may occur between any two tokens, before\\nthe first, and after the last, without changing the token list; a digit run is\\nnever split, so `12` is one `INT` and `1 2` is two; no token other than `INT`\\nspans more than one character. The lexer fails **only** via rule 5.\\n\\n### 3.4 Numeral value\\n\\nLet `digitval('0') = 0, …, digitval('9') = 9`. For a digit string\\n`d = d₁d₂…d_k` (`k ≥ 1`):\\n\\n```\\nval(d) = Σ_{i=1..k} digitval(dᵢ) · 10^(k−i)\\n```\\n\\nLeading zeros are permitted and carry no significance: `val(\\\"007\\\") = 7`.\\nValues are unbounded (no overflow at lexing).\\n\\n## 4. Concrete grammar\\n\\nStart symbol `expr`; terminals are the tokens of §3.2.\\n\\n```ebnf\\nexpr   ::= term { ( PLUS | MINUS ) term } ;\\nterm   ::= factor { STAR factor } ;\\nfactor ::= INT\\n         | LPAREN expr RPAREN\\n         | PLUS factor\\n         | MINUS factor ;\\n```\\n\\nDerived binding rules (normative):\\n\\n| Level (tightest first) | Operators                  | Associativity        |\\n|------------------------|----------------------------|----------------------|\\n| 1                      | unary `+`, unary `-`       | prefix, right-chaining |\\n| 2                      | binary `*`                 | left                 |\\n| 3                      | binary `+`, binary `-`     | left                 |\\n\\n- Parentheses group explicitly and override the table.\\n- Binary repetition is left-associative: `a ⊕ b ⊕ c` parses as\\n  `(a ⊕ b) ⊕ c` for `⊕ ∈ {+, -, *}`.\\n- Unary operators chain rightward: `MINUS MINUS x` is `−(−x)`.\\n- A unary operator may follow any opening position: start of input, after\\n  `(`, and after a binary operator (e.g. `2 * -3`, `1 - -2`).\\n\\n**[N]** The grammar is LL(1): `FIRST(factor) = {INT, LPAREN, PLUS, MINUS}`\\n(pairwise disjoint) and the continuation decisions in `expr`/`term` are each\\ndetermined by a single lookahead token. Determinism is stated as Theorem U\\n(§8).\\n\\n## 5. Abstract syntax\\n\\nAbstract syntax trees (ASTs) are generated by:\\n\\n```\\nE ::= Int(n) | Neg(e) | Add(e₁, e₂) | Sub(e₁, e₂) | Mul(e₁, e₂)      n ∈ ℤ\\n```\\n\\n- `Int(n)` carries the payload value `n` of an `INT` token.\\n- Unary plus is semantically the identity and **contributes no AST node**;\\n  its only trace is the consumed `PLUS` token (see rule C-Plus, §7).\\n- Parentheses contribute no AST node; they influence only the tree shape.\\n- Each `Neg`/`Add`/`Sub`/`Mul` node corresponds to one operator application.\\n\\n## 6. Evaluation semantics\\n\\n`eval : E → ℤ` is defined by structural recursion:\\n\\n```\\n(E-Lit)  eval(Int n)       = n\\n(E-Neg)  eval(Neg e)       = − eval(e)\\n(E-Add)  eval(Add e₁ e₂)   = eval(e₁) + eval(e₂)\\n(E-Sub)  eval(Sub e₁ e₂)   = eval(e₁) − eval(e₂)\\n(E-Mul)  eval(Mul e₁ e₂)   = eval(e₁) × eval(e₂)\\n```\\n\\nSemantics per concrete construct:\\n\\n| Construct        | Semantics                                   |\\n|------------------|---------------------------------------------|\\n| integer literal  | its numeral value `val(d)` (§3.4)           |\\n| unary `+e`       | identity: the value of `e`                  |\\n| unary `-e`       | additive inverse of the value of `e` in ℤ   |\\n| binary `e₁ + e₂` | sum of the operands' values in ℤ            |\\n| binary `e₁ - e₂` | difference of the operands' values in ℤ     |\\n| binary `e₁ * e₂` | product of the operands' values in ℤ        |\\n| `( e )`          | the value of `e` (grouping only)            |\\n\\n**Totality.** Every recursive call of `eval` is on an immediate subexpression,\\nso `eval` is total by structural induction. Since ℤ is closed under `+`, `−`,\\n`×`, evaluation of a well-formed expression terminates, raises no exceptions,\\nand has no undefined cases (no overflow, no division).\\n\\n**[N]** Python's `int` is an arbitrary-precision integer type and thereby\\nrealizes ℤ; the implementation inherits totality from the semantics above.\\n\\n## 7. Token coverage and the language\\n\\nThe inductive relation `e ⇓ t` (“AST `e` covers token list `t`”) links syntax\\nto tokens:\\n\\n```\\n(C-Int)   Int(n) ⇓ [INT n]\\n\\n(C-Neg)   e ⇓ t                    ⟹ Neg(e) ⇓ MINUS · t\\n(C-Plus)  e ⇓ t                    ⟹ e      ⇓ PLUS  · t\\n\\n(C-Add)   e₁ ⇓ t₁  and  e₂ ⇓ t₂    ⟹ Add(e₁, e₂) ⇓ t₁ · PLUS  · t₂\\n(C-Sub)   e₁ ⇓ t₁  and  e₂ ⇓ t₂    ⟹ Sub(e₁, e₂) ⇓ t₁ · MINUS · t₂\\n(C-Mul)   e₁ ⇓ t₁  and  e₂ ⇓ t₂    ⟹ Mul(e₁, e₂) ⇓ t₁ · STAR  · t₂\\n\\n(C-Par)   e ⇓ t                    ⟹ e      ⇓ LPAREN · t · RPAREN\\n```\\n\\n**Definition (language).** `t ∈ L(G)`  ⟺  `∃ e. e ⇓ t`.\\n\\nThe EBNF grammar of §4 denotes exactly `L(G)`: every coverage derivation\\nreplays as a grammar derivation and vice versa (structural induction; the\\nparen rule corresponds to the `LPAREN expr RPAREN` production, C-Plus to\\n`PLUS factor`).\\n\\n## 8. Parsing\\n\\n### 8.1 Operational reference algorithm\\n\\n`parse : Token* → E ⊎ ParseError` — recursive descent with a cursor over the\\ntoken list; each function returns the built (sub)tree and the unconsumed\\nremainder:\\n\\n```\\nparse(t) = let (e, rest) = expr(t) in\\n           if rest = ε then e else error UnexpectedToken(head(rest))\\n\\nexpr(t) = let (e, rest) = term(t) in loop where\\n  rest = PLUS · rest′  → (e′, rest) ← term(rest′); e ← Add(e, e′); repeat\\n  rest = MINUS · rest′ → (e′, rest) ← term(rest′); e ← Sub(e, e′); repeat\\n  otherwise            → return (e, rest)\\n\\nterm(t) = let (f, rest) = factor(t) in loop where\\n  rest = STAR · rest′  → (f′, rest) ← factor(rest′); f ← Mul(f, f′); repeat\\n  otherwise            → return (f, rest)\\n\\nfactor(t) =\\n  t = INT(n) · rest  → (Int n, rest)\\n  t = LPAREN · rest  → (e, rest′) ← expr(rest)\\n                       if rest′ = RPAREN · rest″ then (e, rest″)\\n                       else error MissingRPAREN\\n  t = PLUS · rest    → factor(rest)                (PLUS consumed, no node)\\n  t = MINUS · rest   → (e, rest′) ← factor(rest); (Neg e, rest′)\\n  otherwise          → error (UnexpectedEnd if t = ε,\\n                              UnexpectedToken(head(t)) otherwise)\\n```\\n\\n**Termination (T0).** `parse` halts on every input: each recursive call and\\neach loop iteration strictly advances the cursor (a token is consumed before\\nevery descent), so the unconsumed remainder shrinks; `parse` is therefore a\\ntotal function `Token* → E ⊎ ParseError`.\\n\\n**Soundness (T1).** If `parse(t) = e` then `e ⇓ t`.\\n*(Induction on `|t|`; each construction step mirrors one coverage rule.)*\\n\\n**Completeness (T2).** If some `e ⇓ t` then `parse(t) = e`.\\n*(Induction on `|t|` using the LL(1) disjointness of `FIRST(factor)` and the\\nforced continuations of `expr`/`term`; the algorithm's choices are the only\\nones compatible with a covering derivation.)*\\n\\n**Uniqueness (T3, corollary of T1 + T2).** If `e ⇓ t` and `e′ ⇓ t` then\\n`e = e′`; the grammar is unambiguous and `parse` computes *the* covering AST.\\n\\nConsequently `L(G) = { t : parse(t) succeeds }`, and `parse` fails exactly on\\n`t ∉ L(G)`. Parse failures are precisely: end of input where an operand was\\nrequired; a token that cannot begin a `factor` (`STAR`, `RPAREN`); a missing\\n`RPAREN` for an opened group; leftover tokens after the top-level `expr`.\\n\\n**[N]** Any conforming parser must be observationally equivalent to the\\nalgorithm above (same accept/reject decision and same AST on acceptance) or,\\nmore weakly, satisfy §9–§10 directly.\\n\\n## 9. Top-level semantics of `evaluate`\\n\\nFor `s ∈ String`, with `lex` (§3.3), `parse` (§8.1), `eval` (§6):\\n\\n```\\nevaluate(s) = eval(parse(t))    if lex(s) = t  and  t ∈ L(G)\\nevaluate(s) = ↯ ValueError      if lex(s) fails (LexError)\\nevaluate(s) = ↯ ValueError      if lex(s) = t and t ∉ L(G)\\n```\\n\\nThe value case is well-defined because the covering AST is unique (T3).\\n\\nNormative contract:\\n\\n- **C1 (Termination).** `evaluate(s)` halts for every `s ∈ String`.\\n- **C2 (Result type).** `evaluate(s)` returns a Python `int` (equivalently, a\\n  value of ℤ) iff `lex(s)` succeeds and `lex(s) ∈ L(G)`; the value equals\\n  `eval(e)` for the unique `e` covering `lex(s)`.\\n- **C3 (Error exactness).** `evaluate(s)` raises `ValueError` iff `lex(s)`\\n  fails or `lex(s) ∉ L(G)`. No other exception may escape for `str` inputs\\n  within the resource envelope of §12.\\n- **C4 (Messages).** Exception *messages* are not contractual. Indicative\\n  strings of the reference implementation:\\n\\n  | Condition                                   | Indicative message                     |\\n  |---------------------------------------------|----------------------------------------|\\n  | invalid character `c` at 0-based index `i`  | `Invalid character {c!r} at position {i}` |\\n  | operand expected at end of input            | `Unexpected end of expression`         |\\n  | token where an operand was expected         | `Unexpected token {tok!r}`             |\\n  | group not closed                            | `Missing closing parenthesis`          |\\n  | leftover tokens after the top-level `expr`  | `Unexpected token {tok!r}`             |\\n\\n  **[N]** The reference implementation lexes the whole string before parsing,\\n  so a lex error anywhere in `s` is reported even where a parse error would\\n  occur earlier positionally. Since both surface as `ValueError`, this\\n  precedence is unobservable at the level of C2/C3.\\n\\n## 10. Verification obligations\\n\\nAn implementation conforms to this specification iff it satisfies C1–C4 and\\nproperties P1–P9 below. Each property is stated so it can be discharged\\ndirectly (e.g. by proof or mechanized testing against the oracle of §11).\\n\\n- **P1 (Totality & outcome type).** For every `s ∈ String`, evaluating\\n  `evaluate(s)` halts and either yields an integer or raises `ValueError`;\\n  no other exception escapes.\\n- **P2 (Purity & determinism).** The outcome (value or exception) is a\\n  function of `s` alone; evaluation has no side effects.\\n- **P3 (Semantic agreement — main theorem).** If `lex(s) = t` and\\n  `parse(t) = e`, then `evaluate(s) = eval(e)`. Conversely, if\\n  `evaluate(s) = n`, then `t = lex(s)` exists and `n = eval(e)` for the\\n  unique `e` with `e ⇓ t`.\\n- **P4 (Parser correctness).** T1, T2, T3 of §8.1.\\n- **P5 (Error exactness).** `evaluate(s)` raises `ValueError` ⟺ `lex(s)`\\n  fails ∨ `¬∃e. e ⇓ lex(s)`.\\n- **P6 (Whitespace insensitivity).** If `lex(s) = lex(s′)` then\\n  `evaluate(s)` and `evaluate(s′)` agree as outcomes (same value, or both\\n  raise `ValueError`).\\n- **P7 (Arithmetic laws).** For all `e, e₁, e₂, e₃ ∈ E` (immediate\\n  consequences of the E-rules and ℤ; useful when verifying at string level):\\n  1. `eval(Add e₁ e₂) = eval(Add e₂ e₁)`\\n  2. `eval(Add (Add e₁ e₂) e₃) = eval(Add e₁ (Add e₂ e₃))`\\n  3. `eval(Add e (Int 0)) = eval(e)`\\n  4. `eval(Mul e₁ e₂) = eval(Mul e₂ e₁)`\\n  5. `eval(Mul (Mul e₁ e₂) e₃) = eval(Mul e₁ (Mul e₂ e₃))`\\n  6. `eval(Mul e₁ (Add e₂ e₃)) = eval(Add (Mul e₁ e₂) (Mul e₁ e₃))`\\n  7. `eval(Neg (Neg e)) = eval(e)`\\n  8. `eval(Neg (Add e₁ e₂)) = eval(Add (Neg e₁) (Neg e₂))`\\n  9. `eval(Sub e₁ e₂) = eval(Add e₁ (Neg e₂))`\\n  10. `eval(Mul e (Int 1)) = eval(e)` and `eval(Mul e (Int 0)) = 0`\\n- **P8 (Precedence & associativity, string level).** For any well-formed\\n  sub-expressions `a, b, c` with values `α = evaluate(a)`, `β = evaluate(b)`,\\n  `γ = evaluate(c)`:\\n  | Source text      | Required value        |\\n  |------------------|-----------------------|\\n  | `a + b * c`      | `α + β·γ`             |\\n  | `(a + b) * c`    | `(α + β)·γ`           |\\n  | `a - b - c`      | `(α − β) − γ`         |\\n  | `a * b * c`      | `(α·β)·γ`             |\\n  | `a + b + c`      | `(α + β) + γ`         |\\n  | `-a * b`         | `(−α)·β`              |\\n  | `a * -b`         | `α·(−β)`              |\\n  | `--a`            | `α`                   |\\n  | `-(a + b)`       | `−(α + β)`            |\\n- **P9 (Lexical corner cases).** For every digit string `d`:\\n  `evaluate(d) = val(d)` (including leading zeros). `12` is a single `INT`\\n  (hence `evaluate(\\\"1 2\\\")` is a `ValueError`), non-ASCII digits are invalid\\n  characters, and every operator/parenthesis token is exactly one character.\\n\\n## 11. Examples — normative test oracle\\n\\nValid inputs (left column is input **as written**, including whitespace):\\n\\n| Input                        | Result | Rationale                        |\\n|------------------------------|-------:|----------------------------------|\\n| `1 + 2 * 3`                  | 7      | precedence of `*`                |\\n| `(1 + 2) * 3`                | 9      | grouping                         |\\n| `10 - 4 - 3`                 | 3      | left associativity               |\\n| `2 * (3 + 4) - 5`            | 9      |                                  |\\n| `100`                        | 100    | bare literal                     |\\n| `0`                          | 0      |                                  |\\n| `007`                        | 7      | leading zeros (§3.4)             |\\n| ` 42 `                       | 42     | surrounding whitespace           |\\n| `-3 + 5`                     | 2      | unary minus                      |\\n| `1 - -2`                     | 3      | binary `-` then unary `-`        |\\n| `--5`                        | 5      | chained unary                    |\\n| `-+5`                        | −5     | chained unary                    |\\n| `2 * -3`                     | −6     | unary after binary operator      |\\n| `-(2 + 3)`                   | −5     | unary on a group                 |\\n| `((2))`                      | 2      | nested parentheses               |\\n| `2*3+4*5`                    | 26     | no whitespace                    |\\n| `( 1 + 2 ) * ( 3 - 4 )`      | −3     | whitespace inside parentheses    |\\n| `(((1+2)*(3+4)))`            | 21     |                                  |\\n\\nInvalid inputs (each must raise `ValueError`):\\n\\n| Input       | Reason                                        |\\n|-------------|-----------------------------------------------|\\n| `\\\"\\\"`        | no tokens; operand expected                   |\\n| `\\\"   \\\"`     | whitespace only; operand expected             |\\n| `\\\"1 +\\\"`     | operand expected at end of input              |\\n| `\\\"*\\\"`       | `STAR` cannot begin a factor                  |\\n| `\\\"()\\\"`      | `RPAREN` cannot begin a factor inside a group |\\n| `\\\"(1+2\\\"`    | missing `RPAREN`                              |\\n| `\\\"1)\\\"`      | leftover `RPAREN` after top-level `expr`      |\\n| `\\\"1 2\\\"`     | leftover `INT` after top-level `expr`         |\\n| `\\\"1 + * 2\\\"` | `STAR` where an operand was expected          |\\n| `\\\"1 & 2\\\"`   | invalid character `&`                         |\\n| `\\\"a+b\\\"`     | invalid character `a`                         |\\n| `\\\"٣ + 1\\\"`   | non-ASCII digit is an invalid character       |\\n\\n## 12. Non-normative implementation notes\\n\\n- **Recursion depth.** The reference parser is recursive descent, so stack\\n  depth grows linearly with nesting depth (parentheses, unary chains).\\n  Inputs nested beyond CPython's recursion limit (~10³) may raise\\n  `RecursionError`. This is a resource limit of the runtime, not part of the\\n  semantics; P1/C1 apply within this envelope.\\n- **Integers.** Python `int` is arbitrary precision; the implementation\\n  realizes the unbounded semantics of §6 without extra machinery.\\n- **Error precedence.** See the **[N]** note in §9 (lex-before-parse).\\n- **Internal names.** `_tokenize` / `_Parser` may be refactored freely;\\n  conformance is judged solely by §9–§11.\\n\",\"path\":\"spec.md\"}"